course: everyday
lesson: 492
title: Friends who only come for the pool: rich kids and rumours
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: After hearing a kid call him «mid» (boring), Monty in Los Angeles decides to test his friends. He tells everyone the pool's closed for a month. Twelve kids used to come over every Saturday; now only six turn up, and they play basketball in the drive and laugh just as much. Monty is sad, but now he knows who his real friends are. Testing friends with a trick is a bit sneaky, though; he could just have noticed who stayed when there was nothing to get.
explain: Some kids use a rich kid's money. In Toronto, kids ask Jasper to buy them snacks every day, and he does. Then they ask for game credit. He feels used. Real friends don't expect you to pay for them, so Jasper can say, «Nah, not today, eh» («eh» is the Canadian way of ending a sentence), and see who's still around. If they leave, they weren't real friends.
explain: Rumours stick to rich families. A girl says, «Lottie's mum has a nanny so she doesn't have to look after her.» But Lottie's mum works and loves her; a nanny helps a busy parent, she doesn't replace love. In Dublin, Declan's mate sneers, «Your da's a gangster, that's why you're loaded» («loaded» means very rich). Declan's dad is a surgeon. «He's a doctor, ye eejit» («eejit» is Irish for idiot). Rumours about where money comes from can be cruel; say the truth calmly, and tell a teacher if it keeps going.
explain: Rich kids can make others feel small without meaning to, and they can make others feel at home. In Bath, the teacher says the trip will cost £5 and Toby shouts, «That's nothing!» Some kids go quiet, because for some families £5 is a lot. In Hampshire, Poppy gives the whole class designer pencils for Christmas; some kids are thrilled, some are embarrassed that they can't give back. Meanwhile in Melbourne, Delphine feels shy at a sleepover in Sienna's huge house, until Sienna grins, «It's just a house, mate.» A good host matters more than the house.
explain: Not every rich family is the same. In Chelsea, Cosmo's parents could give him loads of pocket money, but they give him £5, the same as his friends, to keep life normal. In New Zealand, Willow's family owns a big farm and her friends think she has everything, but her dad works from dawn to dusk and she misses him. Money doesn't mean no problems. And in Limerick, Aoife, whose family has little money, is invited to a rich classmate's party at a fancy hotel. Mam says, «We can't do a present for that.» Aoife says, «Wee card then» («wee» means small). She goes with a card and has a great time: going and being friendly is what counts.
fact: Some kids stay around a rich child only for their things; real friends stay when there's nothing to get.
fact: Testing friends with a trick can show who cares, but it is a bit sneaky; noticing who stays works without tricks.
fact: Real friends don't expect you to pay for them; you can say no, and anyone who leaves wasn't a real friend.
fact: A nanny helps a busy parent; it does not mean the parent doesn't love the child.
fact: Rumours about where a family's money comes from can be cruel; say the truth calmly and tell a teacher if it keeps going.
fact: Some rich parents keep pocket money ordinary on purpose, to teach the value of money.
fact: A good host makes guests feel at home, and that matters more than the size of the house.
fact: For some families £5 is a lot, so it's kind to think before saying something is cheap.
fact: Big gifts can make others feel they owe you; small or equal gifts keep things comfortable.
fact: Having money doesn't mean having no problems; rich children can feel lonely too.
fact: A rich child usually just wants their friends at the party; a card is a fine present.
fact: Words in this lesson: «mid» means boring, «eh» ends a sentence in Canada, «loaded» means very rich, «eejit» (Ireland) means idiot, «wee» (Scotland, Ireland) means small, «mate» means friend.
exercise: How did Monty test his friends? | Said the pool was closed for a month | Asked each of them for money to swim | Moved his family to a new house | Filled the pool up with jelly
exercise: Twelve kids came on Saturdays and half stopped. How many still came? | Six | Twelve | Three | Eight
exercise: Why is Monty's test «a bit sneaky»? | It's a trick, not the truth | The pool was closed for real | He told his mum a lie as well | Pools are never closed in LA
exercise: What could Monty have done instead of a trick? | Noticed who stayed with nothing to get | Bought even more cool stuff for friends | Closed the pool for good, for a year | Asked a teacher to pick his friends
exercise: Kids ask Jasper for snacks, then game credit. What should he do? | Say no and see who stays | Buy them even more stuff | Give them his bank card | Move to a different school
exercise: «Nah, not today, eh.» Where does «eh» at the end come from? | Canada | Scotland | Texas | Wales
exercise: «Lottie's mum has a nanny so she doesn't have to look after her.» What's the truth? | Her mum works, loves her, and has help | Her mum really doesn't want her at all | The nanny is secretly Lottie's real mum | Her mum is away on holiday all the year
exercise: What is Declan's dad's real job? | A surgeon | A gangster | A banker | A footballer
exercise: «Ye eejit.» What does Declan call his mate? | An idiot | A genius | A thief | A liar
exercise: What's the wise move if the gangster rumour keeps going? | Say the truth calmly and tell a teacher | Start a worse rumour about the other kid | Get his dad to come and scare the boy | Agree it's true so it all stops faster
exercise: Why did some kids go quiet when Toby said £5 was «nothing»? | For their families £5 is a lot | They wanted the trip to cost more | They hadn't heard of the trip yet | They were tired after PE lesson
exercise: Why did some kids feel embarrassed about Poppy's designer pencils? | They couldn't give anything back | They didn't like pencils at all | The pencils were all broken | They wanted pens, not pencils
exercise: Delphine was shy in Sienna's huge house. What did Sienna do that helped? | Said, «It's just a house, mate» | Showed her every single room | Told her how much it cost | Left her alone in the kitchen
exercise: Why does Cosmo only get £5 pocket money? | His parents want to keep life normal | His parents have no money to spare | He spends money on silly things | His school sets the pocket money
exercise: Willow's friends think she has everything. What does she miss? | Her dad, who works dawn to dusk | Her pony, which ran off last week | A pool like Monty has in LA | Her old house in the city
exercise: Aoife can't afford a present for the hotel party. What does she take? | A wee card | Nothing | A big cake | Her mam
test: «rich kids are lucky, they've got no problems». True? | No, money doesn't stop problems | Yes, money fixes everything | Yes, rich kids never feel lonely | No, rich kids have more homework
test: Jasper says no to buying snacks. Two of the five kids stop talking to him. How many stayed his friends? | Three | Two | Five | Seven
test: «Declan's da is loaded cos he's a gangster, everyone says». Is «everyone says» proof? | No, a rumour isn't proof | Yes, if lots of people say it | Yes, rich people are gangsters | No, only teachers can say it
test: In a cartoon, the rich kid buys friends with sweets and they love him forever. What's more real? | Bought friends often leave when the sweets stop | Sweets always make friends that last forever | Real friends are always the ones you pay | Rich kids can't make friends without sweets
test: Toby: «£5 is nothing lol». What would have been a kinder thing to say? | Nothing about the price at all | «£5? That's way too cheap, man.» | «I'll pay for anyone who's poor!» | «My pocket money's £20, so easy.»
test: Poppy wants to give her class something for Christmas next year without making anyone feel they owe her. What fits? | Homemade cards for everyone | A designer bag for each kid | A new phone for her best mates | Gold pens with names on them
test: Aoife: «it's in a posh hotel, I'll look like a right scruff with a card». What's more likely? | Her friend is glad she came, card and all | Her friend sends her home for no present | The hotel won't let in anyone with a card | Everyone laughs at her card for the day
test: What would most likely happen if Jasper kept buying the game credit? | They'd keep asking for more | They'd pay him back next week | They'd stop asking by themselves | They'd buy him credit in return
test: Monty told his friends the pool was closed. Six kids stopped coming, then one came back when he heard it was open again. How many true friends does that suggest Monty has from the twelve? | Six | Seven | Five | Twelve
test: Willow's mate: «u live on a massive farm, u can't be sad». What's true? | Anyone can be sad, whatever they own | Farm kids are never sad, only city kids | She's only sad because the farm's too big | Sadness only comes to kids with no money
test: Cosmo's mate: «ur parents r loaded and u only get a fiver? tight». What's the likely reason? | They want to keep his life normal | They are secretly poor and hiding it | They don't love Cosmo very much | They forgot to give him the rest
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises friends who attend a wealthy child's house solely for its amenities? | They are not true friends, since they value the possessions over the person | They are the truest of friends, since they visit with great regularity | They are merely shy, and will in time come to value the child himself | They are required by courtesy to attend whenever a pool is available
exam: Identify the principal reason why a remark that five pounds is «nothing» may cause distress. | For some families such a sum is considerable | Such remarks are prohibited by law in schools | Five pounds is in fact a very great fortune | The trip in question was in fact cancelled
exam: Given that a pupil is repeatedly asked by classmates to purchase snacks and game credit on their behalf, ascertain the most appropriate response. | To decline, and observe who remains a friend | To comply, lest the classmates depart | To purchase still more costly items | To request repayment with interest
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning rumours as to the source of a family's wealth. | They may be cruel and should be answered calmly with the truth | They are invariably accurate and ought to be believed at once | They are harmless and may be repeated freely in the playground | They are best answered by spreading a rumour of one's own
